{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Wisdom has built her house, she has hewn out her seven pillars; she has slaughtered her slaughter, she has mixed her wine; she has also set her table; she has sent out her maidens to call from the highest places of the city</b> (Proverbs 9:1)." "<b>Shelomo</b> in the verses of this passage (Proverbs 9:4) comes to speak about our faith and about the faith of idolaters, and he compared our faith to a wise woman, and compared the faith of idolaters to the clamorous woman of folly. He compared our faith to a wise woman — that is what he said, ״חכמות בנתה ביתה״; he called the wisdom of our Torah by the word חכמות (wisdoms) because it embraces all wisdoms. And he said that the wisdom of the Torah is like a woman who built her house upon many pillars so that it would be strong, never to be shaken; and the count of seven is not literal, but it is like (Proverbs 24:16) ״כי שבע יפול צדיק וקם״. And behold, she busies herself to slaughter the slaughter and to prepare, and to mix the wine, and she herself sets the table — not by command; this is the sense of אף ערכה. And further, she calls to her invitees, and they are the maidens who are her rejoicing, also to the fool and to the one lacking heart. Now this is a parable for the wisdom of our Torah, which is perfect in every kind of perfection, as it says (Deuteronomy 8:9) ״לא תחסר כל בה״, and it is what teaches the way of unity; and when a man reflects well upon it, there he will find an answer to all who dispute it, so that the Torah is set out and arranged before every understanding person like a prepared table set before the hungry to eat. And this Torah is what gives wisdom to the simple, as it is written (Psalms 19:8) ״עדות ה׳ נאמנה מחכימת פתי״. And it warns all creatures to engage in it and to abandon their folly, which is what is written (Proverbs 9) ״מי פתי יסור הנה חסר לב אמרה לו״, and what does she say to the fool and the one lacking heart? (ibid.) ״לכו לחמו בלחמי ושתו ביין מסכתי, עזבו פתאים וחיו ואשרו בדרך בינה, כי בי ירבו ימיך״ and so on. And he compared the faith of the idolaters to the clamorous woman of folly, who gives counsel to lead the many astray, and who strengthens the hands of transgressors, and who praises folly and evil deeds — that is what is written (ibid.) ״אשת כסילות הומיה פתיות ובל ידעה מה, וישבה לפתח ביתה על כסא מרומי קרת״. Scripture explained that this woman is the opposite of the first: for the first was wise and this one is a woman of folly; the first built and slaughtered and mixed and set the table, and she is silent, while this one does not know how to do any of these things, and she is clamorous — this is ״ובל ידעה מה״. And just as the first woman was perfect in every kind of perfection and diligence, so this one is lacking in all of them, for she does not know how to do anything except that she sits at the entrance of the house — this is ״ושבה לפתח״, for this is in an exposed place where she does not enter within. And how could she enter, when there is nothing prepared there? So too the idolaters do not wish to shelter under the shadow of the Holy One, blessed be He, but they go out from under His authority and did not merit to enter within — this is ״ושבה לפתח ביתה״, in an exposed place where she does not enter within. And thus R. Yehoshua ben Chananyah answered Andrianus: 'The divinities you worship — if you wish to worship them, worship them outside the world that the Holy One, blessed be He, created, in a world you make for yourselves, for it is not fitting that you worship them within His domain.' And Scripture informed us that the idolaters, despite lacking all perfections, pride themselves in their evil deeds, which is what it says, ״על כסא מרומי קרת״ — for above it mentioned ״על גפי״ and here ״על כסא״. And it is written: ״לקרוא לעוברי דרך המישרים אורחותם, מי פתי יסור הנה חסר לב ואמרה לו״, and what does she say? ״מים גנובים ימתקו ולחם סתרים ינעם״. For just as the first, the wise woman, rebukes the fools to abandon their folly, so this one, who is the woman of folly, teaches them to hold fast to their folly. And just as the first teaches the way of life and says (ibid.) ״כי בי ירבו ימיך״, so this one teaches the way of death — this is what is written (ibid.) ״ולא ידע כי רפאים שם בעמקי שאול קרואיה״ — the opposite of the guests of the first." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״חכמות בנתה ביתה״ — this is the Torah, which built all the worlds. ״חצבה עמודיה שבעה״ — it was hewn from seven heavens. If a man merits and engages in it, he inherits seven lands; and if not, he is removed from seven lands. The meaning of seven lands is this world, which is divided into seven climates; and it is as if they said: he inherits this world and is removed from the World to Come." "<b>And this Midrash</b> makes the count of seven precisely, because it is the precise count that embraces the upper ones and the lower ones, and it is the count found in many commandments of the Torah that revolve around the count of seven — such as the commandments of Sabbath, the Sabbatical year, and the Jubilee. For the seventh is sanctified in days, which is the Sabbath; in years, which is the Sabbatical year; and in Sabbatical years, which is the Jubilee. Also the days of Pesach are seven, and the days of Sukkot are seven, and the four species of the lulav revert to seven. Also the days of mourning are seven, like the days of rejoicing — and all of it is one matter, alluding to the seven days of Creation. Also Bilam offered on seven altars and said (Numbers 23:4) ״את שבעת המזבחות ערכתי״. Also the days of ordination (milu'im) were seven, as it is explicitly stated in Scripture (Leviticus 8:33) ״כי שבעת ימים ימלא את ידכם״. And all these things that revolve around seven — their root and their source are from one place, and their origin is from one spring. And the reason Aaron was not inaugurated in the High Priesthood on the seventh day itself, but only on the morrow, on the eighth day, is because the High Priest is uniquely associated with the Sanctuary for the service of the One El; and the count of eight, after the seven, is in the rank of the One. Therefore Scripture commanded that the beginning of Aaron the priest's priesthood and the inauguration of his service be on the eighth day, and this is what is written." "<b>And it came to pass on the eighth day, that Moses called Aaron and his sons, and the elders of Israel.</b> The inauguration of the High Priesthood on the eighth day includes yet another reason, and it is that we find most of the matters of the Tabernacle and the Sanctuary revolve around the count of eight. For the garments of the High Priest were eight, and they are: the tzitz, the choshen, the ephod, the me'il, the tunic of checkered work, the mitre, the sash, and the linen breeches. The spices of the anointing oil and the incense were also eight: in the anointing oil, four — myrrh, cinnamon, calamus, and cassia; and in the incense, four — stacte, onycha, galbanum, and frankincense. Also the staves were eight: two staves of the Ark, and two for the table, and two for the golden altar, and two for the altar of burnt offering. Also the offerings that were brought were not fit to be offered until after eight days, as it is written (Leviticus 22:27) ״ומיום השמיני והלאה ירצה״. Also the song that the Levites would sing over the offerings was in eight instruments (i.e., eight kinds of musical instruments): למנצח, על נגינות, על מחלת, על עלמות, על הנחילות, על השושנים, על הגתית, על השמינית. This eighth day was the first of Nisan, on which the Tabernacle was erected, and it took ten crowns, meaning ten kinds of honor and distinction were gathered on that day: it was first for the year of their going out from Egypt; first for the priesthood of Aaron, on which he was inaugurated and on which his priesthood began; first for the offerings; first that Israel was blessed with the Priestly Blessing; first for the descent of fire from heaven upon the copper altar; first for the new-moon goat-offering; first for slaughtering outside (the Sanctuary), for on it offerings were prohibited to be brought unless they first brought them to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting; first for the indwelling of the Shekhinah; first for the offerings of the princes, on which Nachshon ben Aminadav offered his offering; first for sending the impure out of the camp of Israel. And thus the language of Torat Kohanim: 'That day took ten crowns: first for the act of Creation, first for the princes, first for the priesthood, first for the divine service, first for the descent of the fire, first for eating consecrated things, first for the new-moon days, first for the dwelling of the Shekhinah in Israel, first for blessing Israel.'" "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ״ויהי ביום השמיני״ — this is what Scripture says (Psalms 75:5): ״אמרתי להוללים אל תהולו״ — I said to the boasters, do not boast. לחוללים אל תחולו — to those who dance, do not dance — the matter of dances, as in (Song of Songs 7:1) ״כמחולת המחנים״, (Judges 21:21) לחול במחולות — to dance in dances. For joy is not granted to a man in this world in abiding measure: not everyone who rejoices today rejoices tomorrow, and not everyone who is troubled today is troubled tomorrow. This is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 2:2): ״לשחוק אמרתי מהולל״. Know that this is so, for in the creation of the world there was great joy before the Holy One, blessed be He, as it says (Psalms 104:31) ״יהי כבוד ה׳ לעולם ישמח ה׳ במעשיו״, and it is written (Genesis 1:31) ״וירא אלהים את כל אשר עשה והנה טוב מאד״. Yet what is written at the end? (ibid. 6) ״וינחם ה׳״ etc. ״ויתעצב אל לבו״. Therefore it says (Psalms 75:5) ״אמרתי להוללים אל תהלו״ — I said to the boasters, do not boast; אמרתי לחוללים — I said to the dancers. And if the Holy One, blessed be He, did not linger in His joy, how much more so the children of men. <b>Abraham</b> rejoiced with great joy: he grew great and slew many kings, and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him a son in his old age. Yet in the end He said to him (Genesis 22:2) ״קח נא את בנך את יחידך״ etc. He returned from Mount Moriah to bury Sarah and sought a place for burial and found none until he purchased it for four hundred shekels of silver. <b>Isaac</b> rejoiced with great joy: they said to him (ibid. 26) ״ראה ראינו כי היה ה׳ עמך״; he was saved from the sword and from the men of Gerar. Yet in the end (ibid. 27) ״ותכהין עיניו״. And if Isaac, who was the Holy One, blessed be He's, own burnt-offering, did not linger in his joy, how much more so the children of men. <b>Jacob</b> rejoiced with great joy: he saw a ladder with angels of Elohim ascending and descending on it, and behold, YHWH stood over him. Yet in the end many troubles befell him: the trouble of Laban, the trouble of Esau, the trouble of Joseph, the trouble of Dinah. And if Jacob the righteous, of whom the Holy One, blessed be He, said (Isaiah 49:3) ״ישראל אשר בך אתפאר״, did not linger in his joy, how much more so the children of men. <b>Joshua</b> rejoiced with great joy: he caused Israel to inherit the land, slew thirty-one kings, and all Israel gave him honor and said to him (Joshua 1:18) ״כל איש אשר ימרה את פיך ולא ישמע את דבריך לכל אשר תצונו יומת״. Yet in the end (ibid. 24) he grew old [and did not merit] and died without sons. And if Joshua, who did not merit to grow old and died without sons — the righteous are thus, how much more so the wicked. <b>Eli the priest</b> rejoiced with great joy, for he was ruler, priest, and head of the court, as it says (I Samuel 1:9) ״ועלי הכהן יושב על הכסא על מזוזת היכל ה׳״. See what is written (ibid. 4) ״ויהי כהזכירו את ארון האלהים ויפול מעל הכסא אחורנית ותשבר מפרקתו וימות״, and it is written ״ושני בני עלי מתו חפני ופנחס״. And if Eli the righteous fared thus, how much more so the wicked. <b>Elisheva daughter of Amminadav</b> — there is no man or woman in the world who witnessed joys as great as hers: she saw her husband as high priest, and Moses her brother-in-law as king and prophet, and her two sons as deputy high priests, and her brother Nahshon son of Amminadav as chief of all the princes of Israel. Yet she did not linger in her joy, for her two sons entered to offer a sacrifice and came out burned. Therefore it says ״אמרתי להוללים אל תהולו״." "<b>And Moses called Aaron and his sons.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted this calling as an act of elevation. For thus Moses said to Aaron: My brother, the Holy One, blessed be He, wishes to appoint you high priest. He said to him: You labored in the work of the Tabernacle and I become high priest? He said to him: Even though you become high priest, I rejoice as though I myself had been made high priest — for just as you rejoiced in my greatness, so I rejoice in your greatness. And when did he rejoice? At the hour when the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him (Exodus 3:10) ״ועתה לכה ואשלחך אל פרעה״ — I said to Him, ״שלח נא ביד תשלח״, remove the mission from me, for my brother is greater than I. He said to him: Even so, ״וראך ושמח בלבו״. Rabbi Shimon son of Yochai said: The heart that rejoiced in his brother's greatness shall wear the Urim and Tummim, as it says (Exodus 28:30) ״ונשא אהרן את משפט בני ישראל על לבו״. Therefore, during all seven days when Moses was occupied with the Tabernacle, throwing the blood and burning the incense, the Holy One, blessed be He, said: Moses, do you suppose that you will become high priest? Call Aaron, that he may serve in the high priesthood. And this is what is written: ״קרא משה לאהרן ולבניו״ etc. Why did he call the elders of Israel? To elevate him in their presence. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Arise, call the elders and appoint him in their presence, so that they should not say he became high priest on his own."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {5 "The verse citation at the head gives Psalms 75:5, but the printed text writes תהילים ע״ה:ה׳ which is Psalm 75:5; the word אל תהולו at the end of the verse is normalized to אל תהלו when repeated, following the text as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:01.490301050Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}